# The third-party tools the workflows run as pinned binaries: the one place their versions and checksums live
# (tests/github_workflows.test.py fails when a workflow names one of these versions itself). The workflows never
# download anything directly: .github/scripts/fetch-tool.sh fetches a tool from its GitHub release, checks its
# sha256 against this file, then checks the project's own signature (a Sigstore bundle, or SLSA provenance with
# slsa-verifier) before the tool is used. Every version here was at least 7 days old when it was pinned, as the
# dependency policy asks (.npmrc min-release-age, backend/lock-requirements.sh).
#
# How each line was obtained, so a bump can be checked the same way:
#   syft / grype      <tool>_<version>_checksums.txt from the release (signed: its .sigstore.json bundle)
#   cosign            cosign_checksums.txt from the release (signed: its .sigstore.json bundle)
#   scorecard         scorecard_checksums.txt from the release (plus SLSA provenance, multiple.intoto.jsonl)
#   osv-scanner       sha256 of the release binary (the release carries SLSA provenance, no checksum file)
#   slsa-verifier     SHA256SUM.md in the slsa-verifier repository at the release
# cosign's and slsa-verifier's hashes are the trust roots: everything else is verified with those two.

SYFT_VERSION=1.55.0
SYFT_SHA256_LINUX_AMD64=bb65249542db5fedeeb4d3f9b018b75e7264dbfbbb10486f044581a92f90490d
SYFT_SHA256_LINUX_ARM64=929bd163db2d7cd2255d3bcb8967208079813d7b46b0c952f7b794d8e2c7d304
SYFT_SHA256_DARWIN_ARM64=1217c258bd5eb91ef3189cf22a82e02f9cac07e4ce934350f428e93aff674625
SYFT_SHA256_DARWIN_AMD64=e2e5831cbabaec9a36fe65c34755c1113982c842a685edce3ce0b54a0ded8003

